          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Involvement in Public Affairs</strong><br>\nDoyle was not just a man of letters; he was also actively involved in public life. He ran for Parliament twice, though he was never elected. He also wrote on social justice issues, which shows his deep concern for societal welfare. His interest in public affairs often intertwined with his work as a writer.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Death</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Doyle found himself immersed in various causes, from advocating for the wrongly accused to promoting the British war effort during World War I. He continued to write until his health began to decline. Arthur Conan Doyle died of a heart attack on July 7, 1930, leaving behind a legacy that would forever impact the literary world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Doyle's Legacy</strong><br>\nArthur Conan Doyle's work, especially the Sherlock Holmes series, continues to be celebrated for its ingenuity and storytelling excellence. His writings have transcended time, with numerous adaptations in film, television, and literature, cementing his place as one of the giants of British literature. Doyle's stories not only entertain but also showcase the power of reason and the excitement of solving the unknown.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
